# R1: reserved interface assessment after G1 and G2

This protocol is written before root opens any reserved observations, outcomes,
application implementation or oracle specification. The reserved interface was
constructed and frozen with T1, but was excluded from T1 collection and repair
design. It shares the generated fixture implementation with T1; it is not an
independently authored application. The evaluator's separately sealed audit
specifies eligibility and task denominators without giving root their semantics.

## Question and fixed implementation

Assess representation recovery, semantic identification, and acquisition on that
reserved interface using the integrated G2 implementation. G1 repaired graph
ownership between the abstractor and its hypotheses. G2 froze section-probe
statistics at the permitted training boundary. Neither repair used reserved
contents. The separate B1 binding-enumeration candidate is excluded from R1.

The implementation freeze must match every G2 runtime file and bind the actual
source HEAD, protocol, measurement utilities and evaluator inventory. The G2 full
suite and five dedicated corpora must have completed and their reviewed results
must be preserved before R1 collection starts. Keep runtime source, policies,
instruments and HEAD fixed through R1 first-pass preservation. A changed file
requires a new phase, never an in-place alteration of a frozen result.

Use the unchanged T1 V2 collector and scorer. Initial candidate generation,
structural restrictions, candidate cap, learning, refitting, contested selection,
fallback exploration, response measurements and identity scoreboard retain their
existing implementations. No reserved-specific learner option or policy hint is
introduced. Learning by that fixed algorithm from the permitted history is
allowed. R1 measures recovery on a reserved generated interface after disclosed
development; it does not by itself estimate a repair effect against original T1.

## Hypotheses and decision criteria

- H1: independent raw-state and action-argument checks corroborate the relevant
  acted-on and related entities, values and bindings on the fixed eligible tasks.
  Report matched, mismatched and unavailable separately. Outcome accuracy cannot
  substitute for identity or binding corroboration. Missing independent anchors
  limit identification and are not automatically learner errors.
- H2: on evaluator-declared expressible relations with adequate initial evidence,
  the learned comparison survives held-out combinations that distinguish it from
  the specified constant or independent-threshold alternatives. Preserve any
  residual lookup or hypothesis-language limitation. A rawly expressible relation
  may still be unavailable under the inferred reading; report both conditions.
- H3: within a matched seed pair, contested acquisition removes every surviving
  explanation for at least one rival outcome on the common evaluation surface
  that untargeted acquisition retains, without creating a forced-wrong outcome.
  This is descriptive evidence, not a significance test. Also report new behavior,
  widened justified uncertainty, failed recognition, and unreachable opportunities.
- H4: independently specified explanations that agree on every permitted
  reachable history remain semantically unidentified. The sealed evaluator must
  supply the scope and argument for any such case. Search failure alone is not
  evidence of equivalence. An ineligible or absent category is reported as such.

The evaluator binds its case inventory, competing predictions, evidence
eligibility, observability limitations and equivalence argument before execution.
Those details remain sealed through complete first-pass preservation. Every
supplied case stays in the fixed task scope, including failed setup and missing
targets. Eligibility does not silently remove attempted clicks from measurement.

## Collection and acquisition

Use `first_pass/reservoir/r1_initial_v1`, `r1_evaluation_v1` and
`r1_candidates_v1` under the transport directory for the new raw evidence and
initial candidates. These names satisfy the unchanged runtime input allowlist.
The initial script and evaluation script are selected by the existing collector's
`--fixture reservoir` option in separate evaluator invocations. Acquisition never
receives either script, the evaluator configuration, application source or oracle
state. The public URL and reset URL identify the interface without supplying state.

First freeze implementation and fixtures. Collect initial evidence once with
seed 1701, prepare the existing bounded candidate set from that evidence alone,
then extend the freeze with both raw initial files and `candidates.json` before
any acquisition or evaluation. No script, hidden ID, cached model or oracle
sidecar is copied into acquisition training. Preserve the candidate omissions
and generator restrictions. A failed preparation is retained and diagnosed; it
does not authorize a learner repair inside R1.

Run four arms in this predeclared order: contested 1701, untargeted 1701,
contested 1702, untargeted 1702. Each starts from byte-identical initial evidence
and receives exactly 60 attempted primitives, including reset and the genuine
observed boundary reload. Use new immutable directories
`r1_contested_1701`, `r1_untargeted_1701`, `r1_contested_1702`, and
`r1_untargeted_1702`. Reset, reload, click, type, select and press all cost one,
including failures. Initial demonstration and evaluation costs are reported
separately. No uncharged replay, oracle setup or special navigation planner is
available during acquisition. Count observation calls and settling as in T1.

The existing collector refits at its initial point and at 15, 30 and 45 charged
attempts; terminal scoring fits the full collected training history. Contested
selection and Explorer fallback are exactly T1 V2. Runtime failures retain their
full records and the existing fallback behavior. Infrastructure or integrity
failures terminate with the partial run preserved. Never substitute stale fits.

After all four acquisition arms end, collect the fixed evaluation trace once,
with seed 1701. Its case resets follow the independently frozen script. Score
initial training and each of the four acquired histories separately against that
same complete evaluation trace and initial candidate file. Evaluation outcomes
never refit any R1 model. Preserve every model opportunity, including failed fits
and missing channels. Root may inspect run status and instrument failures during
execution; no learner or policy change is allowed, and semantic diagnosis waits
until the full first-pass result is preserved.

## Measurements, controls and preservation

Retain the unchanged scorer's point decision-list predictions separately from
RULE and LIST forced-correct, forced-wrong, ambiguous and unestablished outcomes.
Keep complete click and fixed task denominators, action and state failures,
sole-vocabulary qualifications, missing actual outcomes, all learned fields and
pairs, training masks, query literals and representative vouches. The outcome
vocabulary is open; discovery of a new outcome is not an instrumentation error.

Use the existing identity scoreboard across the initial frozen candidates and
report its fixed and current-reading surfaces separately. Record candidate count,
intersection, union, supported coverage, contradictions, surviving readings and
unshared claims. An empty or shrinking shared surface establishes no identity
agreement. Compare actual rival outcomes and their full supporting conjunctions;
counts of removed clauses alone cannot establish acquisition progress. Changes
in representation and query language remain visible in the comparison.

The evaluator-only configured binding adapter is separately frozen and reviewed
using invented records before execution. It reuses the retained T1 control
utilities and checks raw script targets and literal arguments independently of
response accuracy. Its reserved configuration remains sealed. Run it only after
a preservation manifest binds all raw records, scores, configuration, adapter
and retained utility. It never changes a model or contributes data to fitting.
Unavailable identity or field corroboration remains explicit; oracle alignment
is labelled and is not an inferred-identity capability claim.

Preserve all five score files, raw initial/acquisition/evaluation traces, initial
candidates, decisions, process records, output logs, source and data versions,
freeze manifests and evaluator commitments before unsealing the diagnosis.
Every output identity is exclusive. Invalid instruments retain their original
source, result, diagnosis and exact exposure history before a correction is
versioned. A negative outcome is a valid campaign result.

Classify demonstrated failures using the raw and evaluator controls: missing
observations, representation/binding, unavailable language, inadequate evidence,
acquisition, revision, or observational equivalence. Distinguish overlapping
causes from unsupported guesses. The first-pass record is immutable; subsequent
repair uses disclosed R1 evidence as development data and needs a separate fresh
assessment. Continue to the next justified repair and relational-composition
experiment after review and local preservation.

## Resource and process ownership

The user lifted the temporary single-core restriction at 20:01 UTC, before any
R1 freeze or collection. Root again coordinates up to 12 logical CPU workers,
using CPUs 12–23 at normal priority for new jobs. Launch jobs with `taskset`
around the existing `run_job.py`; retain their exact inner commands. Numerical
libraries still use one thread and `PYTHONHASHSEED=0`. Shared fixture sessions
and acquisition arms remain sequential in the declared order. After the common
evaluation and service reaping, the five independent score jobs may run in
parallel, within available memory. All must finish before preservation.
The resource change is recorded in `../development/g2/resource_override_v2.json`;
the prior protocol is retained under `instrument_revisions`. No GPU or paid
compute is used.

Each job records source hashes, working directory, times, explicit ownership,
command and termination. The runner reaps its child; the owning agent reaps the
runner session. A fixture service is stopped and reaped by its owner after the
last browser run. Small metadata-only audits may proceed under the same CPU
affinity and priority, without starting another fit or numerical workload.
